{
  /* Every workspace's `tsconfig.json` extends this. Options that differ per
     workspace — `lib`, `types`, `jsx`, `include` — are set there, never here, so
     that `shared/` can be denied the DOM without the client losing it.

     ── Plain per-workspace configs, NOT project references ──────────────────

     Project references are the textbook answer for a TypeScript monorepo and are
     deliberately not used. References exist to order *emit* and to share build
     state between projects, and this repo emits nothing from `tsc`: every config
     is `noEmit`, Vite produces the bundle, and Node strips the service's types at
     load. So references would buy no build ordering that matters.

     What they would cost is real. `client` resolves `@sports-app/shared/types.ts`
     through the workspace symlink and shared's `exports` map — which is also
     exactly how Vite, Vitest and Node resolve it. A `references` array would add a
     *second* resolution graph that only `tsc` reads, so `tsc` and Vitest could
     disagree about what `@sports-app/shared` means while both looked green. One
     resolution path, understood by all four consumers, is worth more here than
     incremental builds of a two-file package.

     ── Why the exports map points at `.ts` source ───────────────────────────

     `shared/` ships source, not a `dist/`. `allowImportingTsExtensions` lets `tsc`
     follow it, Vite and Vitest transform it, and Node ≥22.18 strips the types at
     load (which is why the root `engines.node` says 22.18 and not 22). The
     alternative — compiling `shared/` to declarations first — puts a build step in
     front of `npm run check` and makes a stale `dist/` a way to get a
     confidently wrong typecheck. `erasableSyntaxOnly` below is what keeps the
     source strippable: no enums, no namespaces, no parameter properties. */
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "moduleDetection": "force",

    "strict": true,
    /* The engine indexes into ladder arrays constantly. An off-by-one there is
       the exact bug class this project cannot afford, so unchecked index access
       is an error rather than a suggestion. */
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,

    "isolatedModules": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "erasableSyntaxOnly": true,
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true
  }
}
